Dialogue: 2,0:00:00.20,0:00:27.10,Hook,,0,0,0,,{\an8\pos(540,118)\fad(200,0)}“LiteLLM was compromised
Dialogue: 2,0:00:00.20,0:00:27.10,Hook,,0,0,0,,{\an8\pos(540,190)\fad(200,0)}through a stolen CLI token”
Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.10,0:00:01.70,Caption,,0,0,0,,The attack vector today
Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.70,0:00:03.93,Caption,,0,0,0,,though, is more prolific
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.93,0:00:04.85,Caption,,0,0,0,,than ever of these
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.85,0:00:05.57,Caption,,0,0,0,,supply chain attacks
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.57,0:00:06.97,Caption,,0,0,0,,and we saw that with
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.97,0:00:08.37,Caption,,0,0,0,,LiteLLM today
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.37,0:00:09.29,Caption,,0,0,0,,which was pretty
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.29,0:00:09.64,Caption,,0,0,0,,sophisticated.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.59,0:00:11.43,Caption,,0,0,0,,They compromised
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.43,0:00:12.83,Caption,,0,0,0,,multiple different core
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.83,0:00:13.65,Caption,,0,0,0,,contributors to the
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.65,0:00:16.99,Caption,,0,0,0,,project by basically
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.99,0:00:18.75,Caption,,0,0,0,,getting access to an
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.75,0:00:21.32,Caption,,0,0,0,,OAuth CLI token
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.32,0:00:22.32,Caption,,0,0,0,,using that to create a
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.32,0:00:23.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,new release.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:23.60,0:00:24.80,Caption,,0,0,0,,and then even like
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.80,0:00:25.80,Caption,,0,0,0,,hacking another user to
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.80,0:00:27.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,approve the release
